WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.050
Start with any whole number, including zero.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.610
Rule A adds three and then multiplies by four.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:10.110
Rule B multiplies by four and then adds three.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.180
Which output is larger, and by how much?

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:15.510
Sketch one example before looking for a proof.

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:20.400
Call the start x.

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:24.030
Rule A forms x plus three and makes four copies of

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:24.740
the whole amount.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:26.100
Look at one copy.

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:29.180
It contains an x tile and a three tile.

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:32.660
Four copies give four x tiles and four threes,

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:35.260
so the result is four x plus twelve.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:38.810
In Rule B, the multiplication acts on

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:39.820
the starting value.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:41.540
Make four copies of x.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:43.060
Now add the three.

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:45.310
This time the three comes in once,

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:46.960
after the copying step.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:49.460
The result is four x plus three.

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:51.280
Line up the outputs.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.970
Each side contains four copies of the same x and

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:55.960
one added three.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:57.300
Pair those tiles.

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:00.100
There are three extra threes in Rule A.

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:01.800
Their total is nine.

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:05.350
Whatever whole number we pick at the start, Rule A is

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:06.360
larger by nine.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:08.900
Check the smallest allowed input.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:11.600
Starting from zero gives twelve and three.

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:14.680
Starting from ten gives fifty-two and forty-three.

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:16.660
Each difference is nine.

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:19.990
In both rows, the starting amount changes the two

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:21.480
outputs by the same amount.

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:25.170
The multiplication makes extra copies of the added

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.200
amount when the addition comes first.

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:29.940
Use that observation with two new rules.

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:32.920
One adds two before multiplying by six.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:36.180
The other multiplies by six before adding two.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:38.940
Draw the matching pieces and find the gap.
